const t={title:"MoE (mixture-of-experts) layer",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Replaces one big feed-forward network with many “small experts”, sending each token to only a few of them.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>MoE (mixture-of-experts) layer</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>In the Transformer block, the feed-forward network is replaced by a router + a row of experts (each expert is itself a small feed-forward network).</li><li>The router looks at the current token’s vector, scores every expert, picks only the top k (commonly 1–2, sometimes more), and adds up their outputs by weight.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The experts and the router are all randomly initialized.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>All experts and the router are trained; each token only sends gradients to the experts it was routed to.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Read-only; each token activates only a few experts.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Making a model “know more” takes more parameters, but the more parameters, the slower each token.</li><li>MoE makes the total parameter count huge while each token uses only a small fraction — decoupling “capacity” from “computation”.</li></ul></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Different tokens need different knowledge (code, poetry, maths)</li><li>let each expert get good at one kind and the router learn to dispatch</li><li>empirically it works better for the same amount of computation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: All experts must sit in GPU memory (saves compute, not memory); training is harder (uneven load, instability); the experts’ “division of labour” is rarely as clear-cut as human subjects.</p></div>'},e={title:"Router / gate",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Scores each expert and decides who gets this token.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Router / gate</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Essentially a small table: token vector × routing table = one score per expert; softmax turns them into weights, then the top-k are picked.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Random initialization.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Trained to send tokens to the experts that lower the loss the most.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Read-only; computed once per token.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>We need a “dispatcher” that is cheap and can learn. One matrix multiplication scores all experts, adding almost no computation.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The router and experts train together: good dispatching lowers the loss, and the gradient reinforces it.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It can get “biased”, always sending tokens to a few experts; load balancing is needed to correct this.</p></div>'},a={title:"Expert",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">An independent small feed-forward network (expand – switch – compress).</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Expert</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Same structure as an ordinary feed-forward network, just many copies, each with different parameters. Computed only when selected.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Randomly initialized, each different.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Receives gradients and gets adjusted only when tokens are routed to it.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Computed once when selected.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The same structure is copied many times: capacity grows with the number of experts, while each token’s computation depends only on k.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>During training different experts naturally lean towards different kinds of input (an empirical observation).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: “Expert” is just a name: the division of labour is usually fuzzy, following statistical patterns rather than human subjects.</p></div>'},i={title:"Top-k selection",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Keeps only the k highest-scoring experts and skips the rest entirely.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Top-k selection</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>For example, pick 2 out of 8 experts, renormalize their weights (to add up to 100%), output = weight1 × expert1 output + weight2 × expert2 output.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A fixed rule; k is set by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>No parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Runs for every token.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>This is where “sparsity” comes in: experts not selected aren’t computed at all.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>A small k saves computation; a slightly larger k is more stable and may work better — it’s a trade-off.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: The selection is “hard” and not differentiable; training relies on passing gradients through the weights of the selected experts, plus various tricks.</p></div>'},s={title:"Load-balancing loss",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">An extra training “penalty”: if tokens crowd onto a few experts, it’s penalized.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Load-balancing loss</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Count the share of tokens each expert gets in a batch</li><li>the more uneven, the larger the extra loss.</li><li>Added to the total loss, its gradient pushes the router to “spread the work around”.</li><li>Some approaches skip the loss and adjust the routing bias directly.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A fixed formula, with a weight coefficient set by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Only has an effect during training.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Not computed in use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Without a constraint, “the strong get stronger”: an expert that starts slightly better gets more tokens, learns faster and becomes even more favoured, while the others sit idle and are wasted.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Even dispatching means every expert gets trained, and when running in parallel across GPUs each card has a similar workload instead of one being swamped while the rest are idle.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Too large a coefficient forces the router into “evenness for its own sake” and hurts quality; it needs tuning, and everyone does it differently.</p></div>'},n={title:"Sparse activation: saves compute, not memory",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Each token only computes a few experts, but all experts have to stand by in GPU memory.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Sparse activation: saves compute, not memory</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labMoe" data-lv="T5">🧪 MoE dispatch (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Say 2 of 8 experts are picked: each token’s feed-forward computation is about 1/4 of “using them all”, but the parameters of all 8 experts must be loaded (you don’t know whom the next token will pick).</li><li>That’s why MoE models are often “huge in total parameters, smaller in active parameters”.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Determined by the architecture.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>No parameters involved.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Deployment needs enough GPU memory; experts are often spread across several GPUs.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Computation determines speed and electricity bills; memory determines how many GPUs you need. MoE trades “more memory” for “less computation”.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>On servers with plenty of memory and heavy request volume, the trade is well worth it.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Not worth it on memory-constrained devices; spreading experts across GPUs also adds communication overhead.</p></div>'},l={title:"Multi-head attention (MHA)",mc:"var(--attn)",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Every head has its own Q, K and V.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Multi-head attention (MHA)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labAttn" data-lv="T4a">🧪 Attention playground (Attention)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>The classic approach: e.g. dozens of heads, each with its own set of Q/K/V tables. During generation every head’s K and V must be cached.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Random initialization.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Each head’s Q/K/V tables are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Every head has to store its own KV cache.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Many “ways of looking” in parallel; very expressive.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Each head can learn an attention pattern independently.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: The KV cache grows linearly with the number of heads, putting heavy pressure on memory for long texts and large batches.</p></div>'},d={title:"Grouped-query attention (GQA / MQA)",mc:"var(--attn)",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Several Q heads share one set of K and V, shrinking the KV cache several-fold.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Grouped-query attention (GQA / MQA)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>GQA: e.g. 32 Q heads split into 8 groups, each sharing one set of K/V, so the KV cache becomes 1/4 of the original. MQA is the extreme case: all Q heads share one set of K/V.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Random initialization.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Trained (a regular multi-head model can also be converted and then trained a bit more).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Smaller KV cache, faster generation.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The bottleneck in generation is often the memory bandwidth for “reading the KV cache”, not computation. Fewer copies of K/V lighten the load directly.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The variety of “questions” (Q heads) is kept while “the labels and content being looked up” (K/V) are shared; empirically the quality loss is small.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Sharing too much (as in MQA) may cost some quality; how many groups is an empirical trade-off.</p></div>'},o={title:"Long-context techniques",mc:"#0891b2",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Letting the model read further: position-encoding extrapolation, sparse/sliding-window attention, long-text continued training and more.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Long-context techniques</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Common techniques: adjust rotary position embedding to suit longer distances</li><li>let each token look only at a nearby stretch (a sliding window) plus a few global tokens</li><li>use more memory-efficient ways of computing attention</li><li>continue training on long texts.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Mostly choices of architecture and training method.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Usually requires some additional training on long-text data.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Helps with long documents, long conversations and large codebases.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The computation of standard attention grows with the square of length, so going longer requires “cutting corners” or optimized implementations.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>A lot of information only relates to what’s nearby, and distant text needs only a little “summary-style” attention</li><li>hardware-friendly implementations also save a great deal of memory.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: “Fits in” doesn’t mean “used well”: details in the middle of long texts may be overlooked; results vary by model and task.</p></div>'},r={title:"Distillation",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Has a small model (the student) imitate the outputs of a large model (the teacher).</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Distillation</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="specCard" data-lv="T5">▶ Speculative decoding demo</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>The teacher provides not just “the right answer” but the full probability distribution (“good 70%, great 20%”), or a complete worked solution; the student learns to match it.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The student model is randomly initialized or starts from a small model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>The student’s parameters are adjusted to match the teacher’s outputs.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>The student model is deployed: faster and cheaper.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Large models are expensive, and many scenarios don’t need all their capabilities.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The teacher’s probability distribution carries information about “which other answers are almost right”, which is richer than a single correct answer, so the student learns faster.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: The student is usually somewhat weaker than the teacher, and can only learn what the teacher knows.</p></div>'},b={title:"LoRA (low-rank fine-tuning)",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Leaves the original model untouched and adds two very “thin” small tables alongside to learn new skills.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>LoRA (low-rank fine-tuning)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="loraCard" data-lv="T5">▶ LoRA parameter count</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>The original big table W is frozen</li><li>two small tables A (d×r) and B (r×d) are added alongside, with r very small (say a few to a few dozen).</li><li>In use it is equivalent to W + B·A.</li><li>Only A and B are trained.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A is randomly initialized and B starts at 0 (so at first nothing has changed).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Only A and B are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>B·A can be merged into W with no extra inference cost; one base model can also carry several sets of “patches”.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Full fine-tuning means storing a whole copy of the model for every task, which is too expensive.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Experiments show the changes made by fine-tuning are often “simple” (low-rank), so a small patch can express most of the change; few parameters mean less memory and more speed.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: For tasks that need large changes in ability it may fall short of full fine-tuning; choosing r is a matter of experience.</p></div>'},c={title:"Quantization",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Stores and computes parameters with lower-precision numbers (fewer bits) instead of high-precision ones.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Quantization</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labQuant" data-lv="T5">🧪 Quantization slider (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>For example, each number used to take 16 bits and after quantization takes 8 or 4: first find a scale factor, then round the numbers to a few “steps”.</li><li>The model gets smaller and faster to read.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A conversion applied to the trained model (some approaches also account for quantization during training).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Training generally doesn’t change; some methods use a little data to calibrate the scale factors.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Computes with the quantized weights; memory use and read time both drop.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The bottleneck in generation is often “reading the weights out of memory”; halving the bits halves the data read.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>LLM parameters contain a lot of redundancy, and each number doesn’t need to be that precise; sensible scaling and grouping can keep the error very small.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Compressing too hard (e.g. 2–3 bits) clearly hurts quality; a few large “outlier” numbers need special handling; results vary by model.</p></div>'},p={title:"Inference serving",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Deploys the model on GPUs, receives requests, schedules computation and streams back results.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Inference serving</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Its jobs: loading weights, queueing and batching, managing the KV cache, splitting the model across GPUs, rate limiting and billing, and pushing generated tokens to you in real time.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Built by engineering systems.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Doesn’t change model parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Every request passes through it.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The model is the “maths”, serving is the “factory”: with good scheduling the same model can be much cheaper and much faster.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Computing several requests together, reusing caches and generating speculatively greatly increase hardware utilization.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: There are many optimizations and they change fast; the exact effect depends on the system and the load.</p></div>'},m={title:"Batching (continuous batching)",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Computes many users’ requests together, so one read of the weights serves a large batch of people.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Batching (continuous batching)</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Generating one token means reading all the weights once.</li><li>If you compute one token each for 32 requests at the same time, one read of the weights is enough.</li><li>“Continuous batching” lets new requests join at any time and finished ones leave at any time, without waiting for the whole batch.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The scheduling strategy is designed by engineers.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>No parameters involved.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Always running on the server.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>With a single request the GPU spends most of its time “waiting for data” and its compute sits idle; batched, one read of the data computes many copies.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Throughput (total tokens per second) rises sharply and unit cost falls.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Batches that are too large slow down individual requests; speed and throughput have to be balanced.</p></div>'},h={title:"KV cache",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Stores the K and V of every earlier token and reuses them directly when generating a new token.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>KV cache</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labKv" data-lv="T5">🧪 GPU memory calculator (MoE &amp; efficiency)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Because of masking, earlier tokens can’t see later ones, so once their K and V are computed they never change.</li><li>So for each new token, only its own Q, K and V need computing, followed by attention with the cached K and V.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Produced on the fly at run time.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Training processes whole sentences at once and generally doesn’t need it; it is an optimization for use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>One per request, growing with length; identical prefixes (like the system prompt) can even be shared between requests.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Without the cache, writing the 1000th word means recomputing all 999 before it, getting slower and slower.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The computation per step drops from “the whole sentence” to “one token”, so long text generates much faster.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: The cache itself takes a lot of memory (layers × heads × length), which is why techniques like GQA and quantized caches exist.</p></div>'},u={title:"Speculative decoding",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">A small “draft model” guesses a few tokens first, and the big model checks them all at once.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Speculative decoding</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="specCard" data-lv="T5">▶ Speculative decoding demo</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>The draft model quickly guesses the next 4 tokens</li><li>the big model computes all 4 together (taking about as long as computing 1) and checks from the start: correct guesses are kept, at the first wrong position the big model supplies the correct token, and the rest are discarded.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The draft model is trained separately or distilled from the big model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Doesn’t change the big model’s parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Each generation round “guesses several, verifies several”.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>When the big model computes one token at a time the GPU sits largely idle, so “verifying several in parallel” costs almost no extra time.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>As long as the draft guesses right often enough, each call to the big model advances several tokens</li><li>by design the output distribution can be guaranteed identical to the big model generating on its own.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: When the draft guesses badly the gain is small; it needs an extra draft model and memory; the speed-up varies by task.</p></div>'},v={title:"Other architectures: Mamba, hybrids, diffusion LMs",mc:"#15803d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Sequence models being explored beyond the Transformer: cheaper and faster on long text, or generating in a different way.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Other architectures: Mamba, hybrids, diffusion LMs</b></div><span class="status no">🔥 A trained capability: learned from data in training, read-only at use time</span></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 What it does</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ How to use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Mamba (selective state-space model): reads tokens one by one into a fixed-size state whose update parameters depend on the input, so it can choose what to keep or forget.</li><li>Hybrids (Jamba): interleave Transformer and Mamba layers, adding MoE in some of them.</li><li>Diffusion language models (LLaDA): mask the text, then predict the masked tokens step by step instead of writing left to right.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Start from what you care about: throughput and memory on very long contexts, or the generation process itself.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Self-attention compute grows with the square of the length, and generation needs a KV cache; a fixed-size state has neither burden.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Mamba paper: compute grows linearly with length, generation throughput is about 5× a Transformer’s, and Mamba-3B beats same-size Transformers and matches ones twice its size. Jamba paper: fits on one 80 GB GPU and was tested up to 256K tokens. LLaDA paper: its 8B model is competitive with LLaMA3 8B at in-context learning.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: These are paper results; the Mamba paper itself notes it was evaluated only at smaller scales, and larger scales are unproven.</p></div>'},y={title:"Mixed precision (BF16, FP8) and FlashAttention",mc:"#15803d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Two low-level tricks that make training and inference faster and lighter on memory: compute with shorter numbers, and move data more cleverly.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T2">Agent</a> › <a data-go="T3">LLM</a> › <a data-go="T5">MoE &amp; efficiency</a> › <b>Mixed precision (BF16, FP8) and FlashAttention</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ A system-level practice: no model parameters change; it is outer process and rules</span></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 What it does</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ How to use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Mixed precision: store and compute weights, activations and gradients in 16 bits while keeping a 32-bit master copy of the weights for the updates; FP16 needs loss scaling so small gradients don’t become 0.</li><li>BF16: the same range as FP32 and simple conversion to and from it, so hyperparameters usually need no re-tuning.</li><li>FP8: two 8-bit formats, E4M3 (4 exponent bits, 3 mantissa bits) and E5M2 (5 exponent bits, 2 mantissa bits).</li><li>FlashAttention: compute attention in tiles inside the GPU’s fast on-chip SRAM, with fewer reads and writes to HBM; the result is exact, not approximate.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ How to use it</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Compare with the quantization lab on this station: quantization shrinks stored numbers, mixed precision shrinks the numbers used in computation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Large-model training is often limited by memory and data movement, not just raw compute.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Mixed-precision paper: memory use nearly halved. FlashAttention paper: 3× faster GPT-2 training (sequence length 1K) and 15% faster end-to-end BERT-large. FP8 paper: matches 16-bit training on a range of CNNs, RNNs and Transformers without changing hyperparameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It needs hardware support for the formats; the fewer the bits, the more care over overflow and lost precision.</p></div>'},f={moe:t,router:e,expert:a,topk:i,lbloss:s,sparse:n,mha:l,gqa:d,longctx:o,distill:r,lora:b,quant:c,serve:p,batch:m,kvcache:h,specdec:u,xssm:v,xprec:y};export{m as batch,f as default,r as distill,a as expert,d as gqa,h as kvcache,s as lbloss,o as longctx,b as lora,l as mha,t as moe,c as quant,e as router,p as serve,n as sparse,u as specdec,i as topk,y as xprec,v as xssm};
